        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image46.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 35,
    "xml_sha256": "0063c53b2f993e215f5133285fe188c503496512c404195103095354661ce28b",
    "text": "Only a zygote is truly totipotent; it is considered unethical to obtain stem cells from this source.\nPluripotent stem cells, however, can easily be obtained from adult tissues. \nExtra: Stem Cell Ethics\n(More on this in Ch. 18)\nhttps://www.genome.gov/25020028/cloning-fact-sheet/",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide35.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image44.png"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId4",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/hyperlink",
        "Target": "https://www.genome.gov/25020028/cloning-fact-sheet/",
        "TargetMode": "External"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 36,
    "xml_sha256": "62921a9733915f8ca3ea26f9ade2d9a0bb16f2689b44686269d5b1062c60ec53",
    "text": "Extra: Mitosis and Aging\nTelomeres are caps found at the end of each chromosome. Every time a cell divides, its telomeres shorten.\nEventually, the telomeres become so short that a cell will no longer be able to undergo mitosis, and cell death occurs. \nTelomere length thus serves as a sort of “molecular clock” for cellular aging. \nLifestyle factors such as smoking, lack of physical activity, obesity, stress, exposure to pollution, etc. can potentially increase the rate of telomere shortening, cancer risk, and pace of aging.",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide36.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image51.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
