  },
  {
    "slide": 38,
    "xml_sha256": "29066cfc845ad7dd8d44b19767aaa6473e051f337ebbd3b11cb02479c77e0bd4",
    "text": "Reduction of chromosome number\nStages of meiosis \nCrossing over & independent assortment\nMitosis vs. meiosis: similarities & differences\nNondisjunction\nTextbook pgs. 563-572\nTOPIC 2: MEIOSIS",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide38.xml"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 39,
    "xml_sha256": "9bc28398b0c52f57bef16cf6d8245de44f86f2f7b1e95927fb78a32056e39ca1",
    "text": "While \nsomatic (body)\n cells must contain a complete set of chromosomes, gametes possess just half the genetic information of parent cells. \nThis is accomplished through the process of  \nmeiosis\n.\n \nIn humans, haploid gametes join during fertilization to form a diploid zygote. \nMeiosis",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide39.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image53.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 40,
    "xml_sha256": "9c89333ea7cd31689d87d6a2a259ae9406ad3ced28fa50aa5993b0704fe2c075",
    "text": "Meiosis\nT\nhe \noutcome\n of meiosis is different than that of mitosis in three ways:\nProduces \nfour non-identica\nl \ndaughter cells rather than two identi\ncal ones\nDaughter cells have \nhalf the \nchromosomes\n of parent cells\nDaughter cells have\n \nchromosomes with different combinations of genes\n compared to those in parent cells\nThis is \nachieved\n through crossing over (more on this later)",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide40.xml"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 41,
    "xml_sha256": "e6050768f3dfad889d5e7016d43a4acc27a17aacc252eb3d9001a09bdd0760fb",
    "text": "Meiosis\nInvolves the same stages of cell division as mitosis; however, in meiosis, \neach stage occurs twice.",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide41.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image56.png"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId4",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/hyperlink",
        "Target": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VzDMG7ke69g",
        "TargetMode": "External"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId5",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image61.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 42,
    "xml_sha256": "b467d2b7140baafc87b81e14171d541291a6cc2e3123dc24403ba42bd06d2fc5",
    "text": "Like in mitosis, meiosis begins with interphase, or DNA replication. Entering prophase I, then, the cell contains two pairs of homologous chromosome\ns.\nKey events:\nChromatin condenses\n to form chromosomes, spindle fibres form, \nnuclear membrane dissolves\n, etc. \nHomologous chromosomes pair up and align vertical\nly \n(\ncalled \nsynapsis\n) \nand\n \ncrossing over \noccurs\nPROPHASE I",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide42.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image63.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 43,
    "xml_sha256": "ceb36d626e0b1d988112023212b0314780f3b2ba49bdd6a4c04c52bba811e332",
    "text": "Crossing over\n \nis an opportunity to mix genes.\nThis increases genetic variation and diversity which is important for sexually reproducing organisms\nRecall that homologous chromosomes are a set of the same chromosome, where one is maternal and one is paternal origin. These contain the same genes, but may be different alleles, or versions of those genes, such as the allele for ‘blue eyes’ vs ‘brown eyes’",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide43.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image67.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 44,
    "xml_sha256": "0102fdaa7d99145a85f9c37a00f8d69b49562960d00a5d8a5e27557bfda06ac6",
    "text": "Two homologous chromosomes \naligned\n during synapsis is called a \ntetrad\n. Each tetrad contains 2 sets of 2 sister chromatids (total of 4 chromatids)\nCrossing over \noccurs between non-sister chromatids \n(there would be no point to cross over between sister chromatids as they are exact replicas of one another)\ntetrad",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide44.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image67.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 45,
    "xml_sha256": "02e9806cded5014aa9921eccf176bede4280e4c7f457a46249351a11d29c9f92",
    "text": "METAPHASE I\nKey events:\nSpindle fibres attach to the centromere of each chromosome\nInstead of lining up in single file as they do in mitosis, \nchromosomes line up as homologous pairs\n, so that chromosomes of maternal and paternal origin will end up at opposing poles \nThe orientation of each pair of homologous chromosomes is independent of the orientation of the other pairs (\nindependent assortment\n)",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide45.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image66.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
