  },
  {
    "slide": 47,
    "xml_sha256": "d63e12c7cda576957786adabb8d9c185290af89c414188e868cde1623bdeb262",
    "text": "Independent Assortment\nThe following diagram shows the \npossible\n gametes that can be formed from a 2n=4 cell due to independent assortment. \nNow imagine the number of possibilities for a human 2n=46 cell! \nTo be precise, it would be 2\n23\n=8388608 possibilities! AND this is just one gamete (sperm or egg) that can join with a completely random gamete with the same 8388608 possibilities! That would be 8388608 x 8388608 = 7036874418000 ways to make a zygote between two parents. AND we haven’t even considered crossing over!!",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide47.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image64.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 48,
    "xml_sha256": "99e59f8bb9dec3419e8523e8d31fad4041aa7f75eebb1b44a4dbee9297237b8d",
    "text": "ANAPHASE I\nKey events:\nSpindle fibres shorten, \npulling homologous chromosomes apart\nSister chromatids remain together, so that each new pole has a full set of DNA\nImportant:\nAnaphase I of Meiosis I is the \nreduction step\n. This means that the \ndaughter cells that are produced after Anaphase I are haploid\n, meaning they only have one copy of each chromosome (i.e. n=23 in humans)",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide48.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image59.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 49,
    "xml_sha256": "7ec5aed85953e35d0778e6c057f81ac58ea321d59e04d168de9e49d141c11a05",
    "text": "TELOPHASE I (and cytokinesis)\nKey events:\nHomologous chromosomes begin to uncoil, spindle fibres disappear\nCytoplasm is divided and a \nnuclear membrane forms \naround each new set of chromosomes\nEach new cell is \nhaploid \nbecause they contain either the maternal or paternal set of homologous chromosomes (\nreduction division\n has \noccurred\n) ",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide49.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image54.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 50,
    "xml_sha256": "2b8d2fe30c87df1ed1b30651c95c7bf300c8d0d885e174a89010b058b0fd7809",
    "text": "MEIOSIS II\nInterphase does NOT occur before the cells enter prophase II (i.e. DNA is not replicated)\nMeiosis II is a lot like mitosis, with the exception of a haploid cell undergoing division to create identical daughter cells\nCells proceed through\n \nprophase II, metaphase \nII\n, anaphase \nII\n, and telophase \nII \nThe result is four haploid daughter cells with a single copy of each chromosome",
    "relations": [
